SM(215,{"n":"1 Timothy 5","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Timothy 5.mp3","d":2910.9,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Council of God."],[8.16,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.0,"Okay, so we'll go ahead and get started, and when we get started here in just a moment, we're gonna be picking up in uh St."],[29.2,"Paul's first epistle to Timothy, the beginning of chapter five."],[34.64,"Um and we're really I mean, this this evening we're going to finish St."],[41.12,"Paul's first epistle to Timothy."],[43.36,"Um we're now well after the therefore."],[50.48,"In terms of how um St."],[54.4,"Paul typically structures his epistles."],[56.4,"We've talked before."],[57.6,"He lays out some sort of teaching theology, makes some points, right, about who we are in Christ and who Christ is and what Christ has done."],[66.88,"And then there's a transitional word almost always therefore."],[71.76,"He goes from that to uh then applying that to whatever the situation is uh in the place."],[79.84,"Uh now we've talked about already that since this is this is the first one of these that we're going through, but we're about to go through some more."],[88.16,"Um, since these that we're reading now are letters from Saint Paul to one person, obviously in this case St."],[96.72,"Timothy, but also we're gonna see Titus and Philemon."],[100.32,"Um that it's a little different in tone and tenor and subject matter."],[105.92,"Because he's he's not writing to a church community or a group of geographically nearby church communities to address their sort of practical issues that are going on."],[119.2,"Rather, he's writing to a person with whom he has this, as we've seen already so far, this father-son relationship, spiritual father, spiritual son, um, this mentorship relationship, uh, and is uh giving him sort of practical advice, right, about how to uh carry out what is now going to sort of become his office."],[146.84,"We talked last time a little bit more about the offices because St."],[150.44,"Paul did with St."],[151.56,"Timothy. We talked about how a lot of people want to try to date this late and say St."],[156.36,"Paul did write it because the way St."],[158.76,"Paul talks, there are already these structures like presbyters and that kind of thing, and and episcopy um bishops that already exist."],[169.4,"Um, and and deacons, but as we talked about before, those existed in the Old Testament, right, and deacons at the beginning of the book of Acts."],[177.64,"So there's no reason why those wouldn't already exist."],[181.0,"And but uh St."],[183.0,"Paul has been giving direction, for example, these are the type of men, and in the case of deaconesses that he talked about very briefly, the type of woman who you want to have in these different roles as you go and you get communities organized."],[200.68,"And so we've seen how Saint Paul is kind of aware that he has less of his life ahead of him than behind him."],[209.96,"And so he's sort of consciously now making this transition from his role as an apostle to now Saint Timothy is going to be taking over some portion of that work from him and is going to be operating sort of independently."],[226.32,"And he's been focusing on kind of church things."],[230.08,"As we pick up here in chapter five, he's still going to be talking about church and community things, but he's also mixed in some very just practical things for St."],[238.08,"Timothy himself. In terms of how he needs to conduct himself and what he needs to be doing in terms of his own salvation, right, as he's working in the community toward the salvation of others."],[253.84,"And showing how those two are things are going to be linked to each other because of the role Saint Timothy is going to have you can't separate him sort of working out his own salvation from him helping work for the salvation of the community."],[270.24,"These are things this is going to go together."],[274.64,"So that by way of recap, as we pick up here at the beginning of chapter five, he's going to be being super practical in some of his advice."],[285.04,"So unless we have any leftover questions, comments, allegations, things left unsaid, we'll go ahead and pick up in St."],[296.64,"Paul's first epistle to St."],[299.6,"Timothy chapter 5, verse 1."],[303.44,"Do not rebuke an older man, but exhort him as a father, younger men as brothers, older women as mothers, younger women as sisters, with all purity."],[317.2,"So he has just said, I know it's been a little bit for us in real time, but he's just said uh near the end of chapter 4: let no one despise you because of your youth."],[329.8,"Because St. Timothy is a young man, and in ancient cultures, as in most cultures today that are functioning properly, uh, younger people have respect for older people."],[342.92,"Um but what goes with that sometimes on the part of older people is sort of a lack of respect for younger people and their opinions and whippersnappers coming and contradicting it, arguing with you."],[358.52,"So St. Paul has said to St."],[360.12,"Timothy, don't let anyone look down at you because of your youth."],[363.8,"He doesn't say, go and stick it to them and put them in their place."],[367.88,"As we saw last time, he was saying, no, you need to lead by example, show them by your life who you are and why they should respect you and why they should listen to you, and then they will."],[379.56,"And so this is really a continuation of that."],[381.96,"That's why I've gone back in."],[383.64,"And he's saying when when you're dealing with an older man, right, you you talk to him the way you would talk to your father."],[393.24,"Right? Which doesn't mean he's automatically right."],[397.96,"Doesn't mean he's doesn't, but there's always that respect there."],[402.84,"Right. Um, I know some uh I know some priests uh from the old country who refer to all of the elderly men in their parish as uncle."],[419.8,"They'll call them uncle as a way of sort of showing that that sort of respect."],[425.0,"They don't call them father because you know their father, they're a priest."],[428.68,"But they call them uncle."],[431.12,"As a way of as a way of showing that."],[433.24,"But that's that's what St."],[433.96,"That's what rebuke is talking about here."],[435.52,"Paul is getting at here."],[436.48,"If you're a younger man, you don't disrespect."],[441.6,"It's not that they're always right."],[444.4,"You know, you might have to talk to them and you might have to correct them, but you don't publicly rebuke them."],[451.52,"Right? You don't embarrass them, you don't show disrespect for them."],[456.24,"Right? Because that's not how things are supposed to work in a family."],[459.92,"Honor your father and mother."],[461.36,"That's back there in the Ten Commandments."],[465.28,"So you treat them like a father."],[466.88,"Other younger men there, you treat them like they're your brother."],[471.44,"Right? Which means you love them, you try to guide them."],[475.28,"If you're an older brother, they're your younger brother."],[478.96,"You look out for them, you protect them."],[481.84,"Sometimes you gotta knock them back in line a little."],[485.36,"But that's the relationship."],[487.44,"And then the same thing with the uh the women in the church, the older women you talk to and you treat like you treat your mother."],[496.08,"With that respect and with that kindness."],[498.96,"And the younger women you treat as sisters with all purity."],[502.72,"This is um I don't remember if we were actually asked a question about this or if it came up somewhere else."],[509.04,"Everything's a blur."],[510.8,"Um, but in the Orthodox Church, as most people know, if you're gonna be married as clergy, you get married before you become clergy."],[520.32,"Right? And and this is this is part of the reason."],[525.28,"Right? If if you're an unmarried man who's looking to get married, then all of a sudden the younger women in the church community aren't necessarily your sister."],[537.16,"Right. And that causes all kinds of problems."],[539.84,"Right. And so people do not look at their sisters."],[552.36,"Men do not normally look at their sisters the way they look at women who are not their relatives."],[557.48,"And so St."],[558.84,"Paul is here saying you look at them as your sisters."],[562.36,"Right? Meaning impurity."],[564.52,"And not that that didn't just mean, again, we're talking about an ancient traditional culture, that didn't just mean that you don't have impure thoughts about them."],[573.24,"It included that, obviously."],[575.56,"But also that you're protective of them from men who would."],[583.08,"And you not only respect, but you want to protect their honor and their dignity and their modesty and this kind of thing."],[593.32,"So this is we've talked a lot in St."],[596.68,"Paul's Epistles and even in the book of Acts about this brethren language."],[601.08,"The family language that's always applied to the Christian community."],[609.96,"And we're all family and we all love each other."],[612.36,"It's not just that."],[613.8,"Being family in the ancient world meant a whole set of responsibilities related to honor and shame and dignity and protecting people and caring for people."],[626.76,"So it in the ancient world, if you were in a family and your brother was poor and destitute and out on the street, that would be humiliating for you if you were living well and he wasn't."],[641.64,"Or if you were living well and your parents weren't."],[645.36,"Right? This would just be a complete humiliation and public embarrassment."],[649.6,"Right. So this is part of what made Christian communities so amazing in the ancient world is that they treated these people who were not related to them in any way biologically the same way that they treated their own family members."],[668.72,"They made sure that everyone in the Christian community had enough to eat and a place to live and close to wear and the same way you would for a family member."],[676.64,"And they defended them and protected their honor and treated with respect everybody in the Christian community, not just their own family unit."],[689.2,"And uh this is something that we've lost to a certain extent because we've to a certain extent lost this view of family."],[700.32,"Families become very atomized in our modern society, where most of the time when we especially in the US, when we think of families, we think of the nuclear family."],[709.84,"Mom, dad, kids, and that's it."],[712.56,"Right. And that unit is sort of sacrosanct."],[718.24,"Umbody could say anything to anybody else's kids, you know."],[723.68,"Uh I didn't grow up that way."],[725.84,"I had all kinds of adults telling me what to do."],[728.4,"But if you were Dutch, you were a cousin, I guess."],[732.4,"I don't know."],[733.2,"But, you know, we've become so atomized that even at church, we're kind of atomized that way."],[741.36,"Right? There's sort of me and my family, and then I have my business, and that's their business, and we don't get in each other's business."],[751.0,"And uh hopefully we avoid talking about the other and gossiping about the other people, um, but even that, not always."],[759.8,"Um, but there isn't this because we've lost this idea of the big extended family that they had in the Roman world with the Potter Familius, and then there were the uncles and the aunts and the cousins, and everybody was one big unit."],[775.08,"That that was a little easier to transfer over to the church."],[779.64,"Where the church community was in that same category."],[784.04,"Right. So if the the other guy in the church is my brother in Christ, then his kids are my nieces and nephews."],[795.4,"Right, and his parents are my aunts and uncles."],[798.76,"And his right, that we're all part of this big family."],[801.72,"But because we've lost that idea of family, it becomes harder for us to understand the church in that way."],[807.64,"But that's how it's supposed to be."],[811.72,"That's how it's supposed to be."],[812.92,"The kids growing up at the in the church together should look at each other as cousins, essentially."],[820.12,"That's part of the, you know, they're all they're all connected."],[824.84,"And we're all connected to each other, and we all have those responsibilities to each other."],[841.32,"So, verse three honor widows who are really widows, but if any widow has children or grandchildren, let them first learn to show piety at home and to repay their parents, for this is good and acceptable before God."],[856.96,"So we have to keep in mind what I was just talking about here."],[859.76,"What does it mean widows who are really widows?"],[862.08,"It means widows who don't have anyone to take care of them."],[867.04,"Right? That's why the next verse is if any widow has children or grandchildren."],[871.44,"Because we were just saying, if a woman has children or grandchildren, right, for them not to take care of her is a disgrace."],[881.84,"Is a public disgrace."],[883.52,"And so St."],[884.24,"Paul is saying, you don't let that happen in the church."],[888.48,"You don't let someone say, oh, the church will take care of Ma."],[891.84,"The church will take care of grandma."],[894.96,"I can go and do my own thing."],[897.52,"Right? That's completely immoral."],[900.96,"Is what St."],[901.68,"Paul is saying."],[902.56,"You don't let first you let them learn honor at home."],[906.32,"That's what he means."],[907.28,"Right? They're violating the commandment by not caring for their own their own parents and grandparents."],[913.52,"But if a woman really has no way to take care, then you need to take care of her."],[916.8,"Or if the kids or grandkids can't."],[919.2,"Right. That's why he says first."],[921.68,"It may be that they're trying to take care of her but can't quite do it."],[926.32,"Because they're in poverty, you know."],[928.48,"And if that's the case, then let them do that first, and then the church can make up."],[933.92,"The church community can make up the difference."],[936.48,"Right? But he's saying it's important that you don't just let people shirk their responsibilities to their parents and throw that off onto the church."],[945.6,"We've already seen St."],[946.48,"Paul talk about that."],[948.32,"He's he's aware, uh, because he's not dumb, that when the church has this attitude of love and compassion and caring for people, that there will uh from time to time be people who want to take advantage of that."],[964.28,"And St. Paul is telling St."],[965.48,"Timothy, you don't have to be dumb."],[967.88,"Right? Because, but why?"],[970.04,"Because taking the people who are taking advantage of that, it's not good for them."],[974.36,"Our purpose is to get them saved too."],[978.12,"Right? And so them not caring for their mother or grandmother is sin."],[985.0,"They're living in a sinful way."],[987.48,"And so if you don't correct that, that's gonna lead them to destruction."],[992.2,"So it's not loving to not correct that."],[996.12,"Right? That's why you don't let them take advantage."],[1004.12,"Because we'll make sure ma and grandma are taken care of, no matter what."],[1009.32,"But it's also important that we save recalcitrant grandchild."],[1014.84,"And and get him to straighten out his life."],[1023.0,"Verse 5, now she who is really a widow and left alone trusts in God and continues in supplications and prayers night and day."],[1032.44,"But she who lives in pleasure is dead while she lives."],[1037.8,"And these things command that they may be blameless."],[1040.68,"But if anyone does not provide for his own, and especially for those of his household, he has denied the faith and is worse than an unbeliever."],[1050.44,"Um so the person who's really a widow and is by herself, St."],[1057.96,"Paul is saying should dedicate herself to the church."],[1060.28,"So, what's he doing here?"],[1062.52,"He's repurposing the idea of the dedicated virgins at the temple."],[1068.56,"Like the prophetess Anna, who we see at the Feast of the Presentation, who was a widow, and so she lived at the temple and lived a life of prayer."],[1076.56,"So female monasticism develops out of this from the Old Testament."],[1081.12,"This is one of the steps along the way."],[1084.88,"Right. Um he's saying here that this is how a widow, right, who the church is supporting, ought to behave."],[1096.8,"Right? She shouldn't be getting money for her upkeep from the church and going and spending it on liquor and right."],[1103.2,"And she shouldn't be um phandering around town."],[1111.2,"She should. Uh, that that's that's not the purpose here."],[1115.6,"But so St."],[1116.32,"Paul is continuing that, but here it's not at the temp, the physical temple."],[1120.56,"That's not the issue."],[1121.84,"You could do this within the church community."],[1125.6,"This is now part of the community of the church wherever it is."],[1129.12,"And developed from this, there was in the early church, you see, mention of it in the canons."],[1135.2,"There was actually an office, what was an office of widow."],[1138.8,"There were actually widows who did this quite literally, sometimes would live at the church or near the church."],[1146.64,"They do a lot of the things the women who were at the gates of the temple and tabernacle did in the Old Testament in terms of sewing and cleaning and helping and right and all those things."],[1156.4,"But they devoted themselves to the church as widows."],[1161.52,"And the church took care of them, and they helped take care of the church."],[1165.28,"And so there was this uh positive relationship and role there."],[1172.76,"And that's something that is often difficult for people who are widowed, especially people who are widowed young, is kind of getting a sense of purpose and place and where they fit in now."],[1190.2,"Right? And St."],[1191.8,"Paul and the church have a place."],[1197.0,"Have a place for a woman who's a widow to uh fit in and contribute and be part of the community and bless others and be blessed by them."],[1207.08,"Um then at the end, of course, he's reiterating what he said."],[1210.84,"If anyone does not provide for his own, especially for those of his household, now he's talking to the son or the grandson."],[1218.68,"Then he has denied the faith and is worse than an unbeliever."],[1224.68,"Here's St. Paul saying that uh, you know, good works are required for salvation."],[1234.2,"I know, scary, huh?"],[1236.6,"Um, he's saying you have to do something."],[1239.32,"He's saying if you don't take care of your own family, you've denied the faith."],[1247.16,"Does it matter what you say you believe, you've denied the faith."],[1252.04,"So faithfulness for St."],[1253.72,"Paul involves, here's a work that it involves caring for your family, caring for the people that you're responsible for."],[1264.12,"Right? And not only have they denied them, they're worse than an unbeliever."],[1269.04,"Right? Why how would they be worse?"],[1271.96,"Remember what St."],[1273.0,"Paul says in the first couple chapters of Romans when he says that the Judeans who haven't followed Christ and haven't kept the law are worse because they had the Torah."],[1284.96,"They had the teaching of God, they had the revelation of God."],[1288.16,"So when they go out and live the same way the Gentiles do, that's worse, and they're held more accountable."],[1296.0,"Right? Those Gentiles who never heard of the Torah."],[1299.76,"Don't know who Moses was, haven't had all that revelation who lived that way."],[1304.32,"They're obviously sinning."],[1306.8,"But they don't know any better."],[1308.4,"They at least have ignorance as an excuse."],[1310.64,"And these don't."],[1311.44,"So that's what St."],[1312.32,"Paul's doing here, too."],[1315.44,"You have the gospel of Jesus Christ, and you're gonna let your mother or your grandmother like starve."],[1323.44,"That's worse than just being a non-Christian."],[1328.64,"That is far worse."],[1330.32,"You're you're you've been given more, so you have a stronger judgment."],[1335.76,"More accountability. Um so, yeah, I mean, that's pretty clear in several ways."],[1343.28,"It's pretty clear that St."],[1344.32,"Paul is saying that uh regardless of what you believe in your heart, uh you're not gonna be saved if you don't take care of your the people you're responsible for, number one."],[1355.76,"He's very clearly saying that."],[1358.4,"Um, it's very clear that this is the sort of the first responsibility."],[1368.32,"Right? You take care of every person is responsible to take care of themselves."],[1372.56,"He already said that earlier in the book: to work with their hands and provide for themselves, to provide for their family members for those closest to them."],[1382.52,"And then beyond that, if they can contribute to help caring for the other people in the church, the extended, the extended family."],[1391.96,"Right. But in all three cases, right, the goal is to positively contribute."],[1401.32,"Right? So this is ask not what the church can do for you."],[1405.8,"Ask what you can do for the church."],[1408.52,"From St. Paul in 1 Timothy."],[1415.8,"Verse 9, do not let a window, a widow under 60 years old be taken into the number."],[1421.48,"And not unless she has been the wife of one man, well reported for good works."],[1426.44,"If she has brought up children, if she has lodged strangers, if she has washed the saints' feet, if she has relieved the afflicted, if she has j diligently followed every good work."],[1459.16,"And so he gives the same kind of list of requirements."],[1462.92,"One of those is age."],[1464.12,"In this case, it's 60."],[1465.56,"There's actually a canon."],[1468.76,"Try to remember which council."],[1470.36,"I think it's the second ecumenical council."],[1473.16,"Somebody will um actually me, I'm sure."],[1475.72,"But it's one of the ecumenical councils."],[1477.64,"There's a canon that lowered the age from 60 to 40."],[1481.08,"Um but um yeah, that is past her childbearing years."],[1490.32,"And she's not gonna, you know, so she's not presumably not gonna fall in love with some guy and leave the church and run off."],[1496.72,"This is she's at a point in her life where she's gonna commit to spend the rest of her life doing this."],[1501.76,"And then talking about the kind of good report she had in her life previously."],[1507.36,"Right. But so what this also shows us, you know, we saw before when he was talking about, for example, bishops and presbyters and and deacons, St."],[1520.08,"Paul listed off some things that were qualities of okay, here's what a Christian man ought to look like."],[1527.28,"Providing for himself and his family, rearing his children, not being prone to to drunkenness."],[1534.4,"The whole list and quarreling and all those things."],[1537.44,"Here we have a list of what Christian women, right, were from St."],[1543.28,"Paul's perspective doing."],[1545.28,"This is sort of what the model Christian woman is doing."],[1548.4,"And you see, raising their children, well reported for good works, has good reputation, lodge strangers, showing hospitality to people and to travelers."],[1560.56,"Uh washing the saints' feet is not just literally that's talking about humility."],[1565.68,"That she's done humble service as opposed to there, there are different ways to serve."],[1570.72,"You can serve in a way that draws attention to yourself and gets you credit, gets you thanks and praise, you know, for the wonderful thing you did, and there's doing the jobs nobody else wants to do."],[1581.56,"Right. And washing people's feet was about as low as it got."],[1585.76,"So that's the example he he gives."],[1588.88,"Um she has relieved the afflicted if she has diligently followed every good work."],[1593.88,"So we get a little snapshot there."],[1599.48,"Verse 11, but refuse the younger widows, for when they have begun to grow wanton against Christ, they desire to marry, having condemnation because they have cast off their first faith."],[1611.8,"Right. So he's saying precise this is what if it's a younger woman."],[1618.28,"She may decide, oh, I want to dedicate myself to this."],[1621.32,"Yeah, you know, I was widowed, I'm only 28."],[1624.44,"I mean, the mortality rate in the ancient world."],[1627.88,"They're frequently women who are widowed at a very young age."],[1632.52,"Um young widow in her 20s may say, Oh, yeah, I'm I want to go spend the next maybe 50 years living at the church and serving the church, and you know, and just doing this stuff, but then, you know, things happen, they see some young guy."],[1649.72,"They think, well, you know, maybe I'll leave."],[1652.04,"And St. Paul is saying, don't put them in that position."],[1658.44,"Right? Don't put them in a position where they're gonna do something like that and break this commitment they've made to God and to the church."],[1667.0,"That's not good."],[1669.16,"Right? So don't let them make the commitment."],[1672.6,"They're not ready to make the commitment."],[1674.36,"When they hit 60, they're ready to make the commitment."],[1677.64,"If it's a younger woman, they're not ready to make that kind of commitment yet, and don't put that stumbling block in their path."],[1685.24,"Verse 13, and besides, they learn to be idle, wandering about from house to house, and not only idle, but also gossips and busybodies, saying things they ought not."],[1698.48,"So he says, right, they're still young."],[1703.76,"They have a lot of energy."],[1706.4,"They're not going to have enough to do at the church's stuff to keep them occupied."],[1712.56,"Right? If you think about a woman in her 60s or 70s, right, and you give her a place to stay and food to eat and some things to do to help the church, she'll be fine."],[1724.72,"You're talking about a woman in her 20s."],[1727.12,"You're not going to have enough for her to do and this."],[1729.44,"So this is why St."],[1730.4,"Paul says they'll start, you know, visiting people and gossiping and all of this."],[1735.44,"So it's better that they be out in the world."],[1743.36,"Right? And now he's going to give the opposite."],[1745.2,"We're about to get it there for."],[1746.64,"Verse 14. Therefore, I desire that younger widows marry, bear children, manage the house, give no opportunity to the adversary to speak reproachfully."],[1760.96,"So he says, let them, if they're very young, let them remarry."],[1764.72,"Let them get married again, manage a household, do all the things that will keep them busy and occupied."],[1772.0,"Right? So they're not just sitting around in idleness, and Satan comes and tempts them, tempts them to go after some young man and leave the church, tempts them to start gossiping, tempts them to whatever."],[1787.28,"Let them go back and marry again and be fully involved."],[1791.04,"This is remarriage at all counter-cultural?"],[1794.04,"Because some traditional cultures like India."],[1798.16,"If you're a widowed, yeah, that's it."],[1799.52,"So I wonder if that's the case here."],[1800.36,"You don't remarry."],[1803.88,"Not really. Not really."],[1805.8,"And especially a Jewish culture, right, where they had leverette marriage and stuff."],[1810.2,"Women, especially remarrying, was very common."],[1814.12,"Yeah. Yeah. Remarriage was very different for men and for women."],[1824.76,"Men who remarried because they were widowers was fairly common, especially if they had children."],[1833.88,"But when you're talking about remarriage of men, then the divorce thing enters the picture and it becomes a whole other kettle of fish."],[1842.92,"Women, even if they were divorced women, weren't divorced by their own choice 99.9% of the time."],[1850.6,"I mean, they might have been happy to if they did like their husband."],[1853.56,"But they weren't the ones going and asking for a divorce."],[1857.08,"They were divorced."],[1859.56,"They were cut off."],[1860.76,"And so in that kind of situation, a divorced woman remarrying is a little different than a divorced man remarrying when the divorced man is the one who put off his wife to marry someone else."],[1871.24,"That's why you see it treated a little differently in the Bible."],[1875.56,"But especially women who were widowed, it was fairly acceptable for them to marry."],[1881.96,"And a man, especially with children, with young children whose wife died, was sort of expected to remarry."],[1888.2,"I mean, it would have been weird for him not to."],[1891.56,"In ancient cultures."],[1893.52,"That's um if for nothing else than to help raise the children."],[1899.44,"Um, so yeah, it wasn't, it wasn't super counter-cultural."],[1903.28,"Um, this is just St."],[1904.6,"Paul delineating a little more."],[1908.32,"I mean, it was already delineated the role that these widows and that the dedicated virgins had at the temple."],[1916.56,"Just like the other roles he talked about really preexisted even in the old testament."],[1923.36,"But there's there is this transition where now we have churches all over the place and church communities all over the place."],[1929.6,"And so a lot of this is how does how does that work now in this context?"],[1934.64,"So he's really laying out how that role of widow works now in the new context."],[1942.48,"Um, yeah. So he's saying if it's a younger woman, it's better that she get remarried and then yeah, stay be involved actively, right, in life."],[1955.76,"That'll be better for her."],[1956.96,"Again, what's what's he looking toward?"],[1958.88,"He's looking toward her salvation."],[1963.04,"And her making a commitment to the church and then breaking it is not good, not gonna be good for her salvation, and uh her trying to live in some kind of idleness and facing all these temptations to gossip and do everything is not gonna be good for her."],[1979.44,"This is what's gonna be good for her salvation, and so this is how he thinks Saint Timothy should handle it."],[1988.56,"Verse 15 for some have already turned aside after Satan."],[1992.16,"If any believing man or woman has widows, let them relieve them, and do not let the church be burdened, that it may relieve those who are really widows."],[2001.04,"So that first part is St."],[2002.64,"Paul is not just talking hypothetically, he's saying we've already had some problems with this."],[2010.28,"And then finally, that's just summarizing."],[2013.56,"If there, if there are widows in your family, you need to care for them, not just expect the church to do it."],[2021.16,"But the church needs to take care of those who really don't have someone taking care of them."],[2031.08,"Verse 17. Let the elders who rule well be counted worthy of double honor, especially those who labor in the word and doctrine."],[2046.28,"So he's talking about the people."],[2051.24,"Just like they were the elders of the people in the old test."],[2054.2,"The presbyters in the new covenant community."],[2058.68,"They need to be uh especially honored by Saint Timothy and by the community and treated with respect."],[2067.48,"Um especially those who are the ones directly involved in preaching and teaching and right, that element of the life of the community."],[2080.52,"Um verse 18 for the scripture says, You shall not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain, and the laborer is worthy of his wages."],[2089.72,"Um this is the second time we talked before the last time that he quoted that do not muzzle an ox while it trods out the grain."],[2096.44,"He quoted that to Corinthians."],[2098.6,"And back then, I won't go through all that again, but for for our friends who want to divide up the Old Testament law into like the ceremonial and the moral and the civil and all these things, and say certain parts don't apply anymore."],[2113.24,"St. Paul is here quoting an obscure agricultural law about how you treat an ox while it's pulling a plow and applying that to the life of the church and acting like it still applies."],[2125.76,"Right? He says, as it is written."],[2127.68,"I mean, that's about his."],[2130.48,"Um he's not doing some weird allegorical thing with it."],[2135.28,"His point is if you have an ox, if you have an animal that serves you, God commands you to treat it with dignity and respect and kindness."],[2146.72,"And feed it and care for it."],[2149.04,"Well, if that's true of an animal that serves you, how much more is that true for a human who's serving you and trying to help you work out your salvation?"],[2157.84,"Right? Infinitely more."],[2160.0,"So if you don't mistreat a farm animal, you definitely don't mistreat a human, especially not a human who God has put uh in this position of authority."],[2171.84,"And so there's there's two pieces here."],[2174.8,"One is the practical, he is talking about money."],[2177.44,"He is saying you need to take care of these people's needs."],[2181.84,"Which is which is where the I'm a I'm a presbyter."],[2185.36,"Uh I don't have a job."],[2190.08,"Because this isn't a job."],[2192.24,"Right? I the the church takes care of my needs, so I can pay my rent and eat, and my wife can eat, and we have clothes and a place to live, so that we can serve this community."],[2208.4,"Right? And that's what St."],[2210.32,"Paul's talking about here."],[2212.32,"Um that's how it always was in the Old Testament, but he's reiterating that same thing here."],[2218.24,"And the other piece, then, is as he said in the in the first verse, this idea of honor and respect."],[2227.08,"Right. Yes, the people who are leaders in the church are servants and use that title."],[2237.16,"Right. But sometimes folks in the church decide that that makes them like household servants, or like the maid, or like the employees in some business they frequent."],[2252.36,"And so treat them with the opposite of respect."],[2255.48,"Right? Or treat them like employees, like they do have a job and you work for me."],[2261.72,"And so he's he's correcting sort of both of those at the same time."],[2267.88,"Right? Sort of both of those at once."],[2273.08,"Verse 19, do not receive an accusation against an elder except from two or three witnesses."],[2280.76,"Those who are sinning rebuke in the presence of all that the rest also may fear."],[2285.16,"Now notice there's a shift here."],[2286.68,"Remember, he said, an older man in the church, you don't rebuke publicly."],[2295.56,"But if they have this role."],[2299.96,"If they're one of the teachers, if they're one of the leaders of the community, and they're sinning and doing wrong."],[2307.8,"And everyone's looking to them as the example."],[2311.56,"And they're doing something publicly."],[2313.4,"Why do I say doing something publicly?"],[2315.96,"Well, you have to have two or three witnesses, he just said."],[2320.08,"Right? So if he's sinning privately, or if it's just a suspicion or something, you won't have two or three witnesses."],[2326.64,"But if there are two or three people in the church who are witnesses who have seen this, that means whatever he's done or whatever he said, he's done or said publicly."],[2336.72,"And so then he needs to be rebuked publicly because everyone's looking to him as a leader."],[2342.24,"That's part of having this role."],[2344.8,"Right? That's part of having this role."],[2347.44,"Is that uh because everyone is looking to you to set an example, then whatever you do publicly is subject to public correction."],[2361.12,"It could be."],[2362.64,"I mean if it's bad enough and they're unrepentant."],[2368.24,"Yeah. Verse 21, I charge you before God and the Lord Jesus Christ and the elect angels that you observe these things without prejudice, doing nothing with partiality."],[2386.0,"Do not lay hands on anyone hastily, nor share in other people's sins."],[2390.08,"Keep yourself pure."],[2393.92,"So uh he calls in, remember, he was just saying about two or three witnesses?"],[2400.64,"He calls in a bunch of witnesses God the Father, Lord Jesus Christ, and the elect angels."],[2409.6,"Uh, by the way, I get I have to do another sorry, Calvinists."],[2413.84,"Uh does this mean these are the angels who were chosen in Christ to have their sins forgiven before the foundation of the world?"],[2420.8,"Or does elect mean something different?"],[2423.76,"Well, angels presumably."],[2427.32,"These are the ones who stayed faithful, yeah."],[2429.56,"Right? That's what makes them elect."],[2431.72,"Right. Upset. Okay."],[2435.48,"So it calls all these witnesses."],[2442.12,"That you observe these things without prejudice and doing nothing with uh with partiality."],[2448.84,"So it's not just God, but it's God and his whole council, the whole council room."],[2454.2,"Like setting up with Christ there the last judgment scene."],[2458.84,"Right. And why is he doing that?"],[2461.8,"It's not just because it's important, but he's emphasizing."],[2466.68,"That as Saint Timothy judges these situations, he needs to judge the way God judges, which is without partiality, without prejudice."],[2476.6,"You don't let your friends off easy."],[2479.72,"You don't go hard on people you don't like as much."],[2482.04,"You don't let wealthy people off easy and go harder on the people who can't do anything for you."],[2490.6,"Etc. etc. Right?"],[2492.44,"He's so shame."],[2493.72,"Same partiality. And uh do not lay hands on anyone hastily."],[2498.92,"That's not laying hands on them like attacking them physically."],[2502.76,"That's laying hands on as in put into one of these offices."],[2507.72,"Yes. That. He gave these criteria."],[2514.12,"Right? And why?"],[2515.88,"It's not just that it's also not going to be good for the church if you do the wrong person."],[2520.36,"It's also not gonna be good for that person."],[2525.0,"Right? Um It's not just I mean, a lot of churches have have experienced a lot of pain from that won't point to details, but from people who maybe shouldn't have been ordained."],[2543.12,"But in addition to that, those people have experienced a lot of pain."],[2547.52,"And a lot of times their whole faith has been shipwrecked."],[2552.32,"And so for both reasons."],[2554.64,"As we've seen all through this epistle, St."],[2556.48,"Paul's always looking at both."],[2559.12,"Right? He's always looking at both."],[2561.12,"The salvation of everybody."],[2563.52,"Not just one side or the other, and whose side should I be on."],[2566.96,"Because there's no partiality and no prejudice."],[2569.44,"We want everyone to find salvation in Christ."],[2571.76,"As he said back at the beginning of the epistle."],[2574.0,"God wills that all men be saved to come to the knowledge of the truth."],[2581.6,"He says, don't share in other people's sins."],[2584.4,"Not just don't you do things, but don't give approval."],[2590.24,"Right? Don't let things slide."],[2592.88,"Right? Because when you do that as a leader, you're participating by allowing it to happen."],[2598.08,"Remember, he had to come at Corinth and 1 Corinthians about you've got this guy committing incest in your church, and you're not doing anything, and you're not saying anything."],[2608.8,"You can't do that."],[2610.56,"You've got to say something."],[2613.76,"Verse 23, no longer drink only water, but use a little wine for your stomach's sake and your frequent infirmities."],[2620.08,"This is very personal to obviously some gastro-intestral issues St."],[2624.32,"Timothy was having."],[2625.6,"This is not a proof text to use against people who don't drink."],[2631.44,"No. That's exactly how I read it."],[2638.16,"How I read it was."],[2639.6,"Right. It's therefore not an article of faith that we don't drink."],[2647.0,"But it never was."],[2648.44,"For some Protestants."],[2650.68,"Oh, people today, yes."],[2652.28,"But you cannot get that from anything in the Old Testament or New Testament."],[2657.16,"But yeah, yeah."],[2658.68,"This is one more piece of evidence in that regard."],[2661.08,"Yes. Verse 24."],[2665.16,"Some men's sins are clearly evident, preceding them to judgment, but those of some men follow later."],[2672.52,"Likewise, the good works of some are clearly evident, and those that are otherwise cannot be hidden."],[2680.28,"So Paul is making an important point here to say Timothy."],[2687.24,"Again, he's talking about judging and administering these things."],[2690.68,"And dealing with sin in the community."],[2693.72,"There are some people whose sins are really obvious."],[2698.2,"They're out there, they're infamous, everybody knows about them."],[2701.64,"It's very easy to lower the boom on them."],[2704.12,"You don't even have to."],[2705.56,"The community has probably already ostracized them and lowered the boom on them."],[2711.88,"There are a lot of other people, though, St."],[2714.36,"Paul is reminding St."],[2715.4,"Timothy, who uh have just as many sins, just as serious sins, but nobody knows."],[2723.4,"They're not as public, they're not as obvious, they're not as out in the open."],[2726.92,"That doesn't make them less important or less serious, or less of a threat to that person's salvation."],[2733.88,"Right? And so he has to use caution."],[2739.08,"This is uh a verse that I wish more people responsible for preaching and teaching in all churches and Christian communities would meditate on a great deal."],[2758.4,"And I won't go too far to detail on this, but the church has been very good during my lifetime, so during the last half century or so, at very publicly condemning the public sins of some groups of people."],[2773.68,"Not incorrectly. The things they've called sin are sin."],[2780.16,"Right. And been very bad about rooting out and dealing with a lot of sins that aren't so public."],[2791.28,"Until sometime later they get made public, and then there's a big public scandal."],[2796.24,"Right. But they were never addressed until the big public scandal."],[2802.0,"Whereas other sorts of sins and other sorts of people get very publicly and constantly condemned very loudly."],[2811.84,"And St. Paul is counseling against precisely that."],[2815.2,"And why? Because in neither case have you done anything to help the sinner find salvation."],[2836.56,"The person with the non obvious sin that you haven't addressed doesn't get any closer to Christ because he hasn't been found out yet."],[2845.6,"And so St."],[2846.48,"Paul is trying to prevent both of these things from happening."],[2852.52,"Both of these things are the wrong way to go if you're in a position of leadership in the church."],[2859.0,"And you need to be addressing the person whose sins are sort of flagrant and obvious in a way that will draw them to repentance and back to Christ."],[2868.6,"And you need to get to know and find out and spend the time with people to find out who it is who has the sins that maybe aren't public yet."],[2877.08,"And deal with them before they become this massive public public scandal for the sake of all of the people involved."],[2888.44,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole council of God."],[2895.8,"Father Stephen's email address is whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2901.16,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.0,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: finishing 1 Timothy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>1 Timothy 5:1</b>; tonight we <b>finish</b> the epistle</li><li class=\"d0\">Past the <b>\"therefore\"</b> - Paul's usual structure</li><li class=\"d1\">first <b>teaching/theology</b>: who we are in Christ, who Christ is, what he's done</li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>therefore</b> → <b>applying</b> it to the situation in that place</li><li class=\"d0\">Letters to <b>one person</b> (Timothy, <b>Titus</b>, <b>Philemon</b>) differ in tone and subject</li><li class=\"d1\">not to a church or group of nearby churches on <b>practical issues</b></li><li class=\"d1\">to a person - <b>spiritual father, spiritual son</b>, <b>mentorship</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>practical advice</b> on carrying out what is now becoming <b>his office</b></li></ul>"},{"t":146.84,"lv":3,"h":"Paul handing off to Timothy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Last time: <b>offices</b> - people try to <b>date it late</b> because of presbyters, bishops, deacons</li><li class=\"d1\">no reason they wouldn't exist - existed in the <b>Old Testament</b>; deacons at the start of <b>Acts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul has given the <b>type of men</b> (and briefly <b>deaconesses</b>) for each role as communities get organized</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul aware he has <b>less of his life ahead than behind</b></li><li class=\"d1\">consciously <b>transitioning</b> from apostle → Timothy takes over part of the work, <b>independently</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Chapter 5: still church and community, but mixed with <b>practical things for Timothy himself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how he conducts himself; his <b>own salvation</b> while working toward the community's</li><li class=\"d1\">the two are <b>linked</b> - can't separate working out your own salvation from helping the community's</li></ul>"},{"t":303.44,"lv":2,"h":"5:1-2 - Family language, with all purity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Do not rebuke an older man, but exhort him as a father\"</b>; younger men as brothers, older women as mothers, younger women as sisters</li><li class=\"d0\">Ties back to end of ch. 4: <b>\"let no one despise you because of your youth\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ancient cultures (and properly functioning ones now): <b>young respect old</b></li><li class=\"d1\">flip side: older people dismiss <b>\"whippersnappers\"</b> contradicting them</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul doesn't say <b>\"stick it to them\"</b> → <b>lead by example</b>, then they'll respect and listen</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Older man</b> = talk to him as to your <b>father</b></li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't mean he's <b>automatically right</b> - but always <b>respect</b></li><li class=\"d1\">priests \"from the old country\" call elderly men in the parish <b>\"uncle\"</b> - not father, since <i>they're</i> the father</li></ul>"},{"t":436.48,"lv":3,"h":"Rebuke, brothers and mothers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Rebuke</b> here = disrespect</li><li class=\"d1\">may need to <b>talk to them, correct them</b> - but not <b>publicly</b>, not <b>embarrass</b> them</li><li class=\"d1\">not how a <b>family</b> works: <b>honor your father and mother</b> (Ten Commandments)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Younger men</b> = brothers</li><li class=\"d1\">love them, <b>guide</b> them; older brother <b>looks out, protects</b>, \"sometimes you gotta knock them back in line a little\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Older women</b> = mothers - <b>respect and kindness</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Younger women</b> = sisters, <b>with all purity</b></li></ul>"},{"t":502.72,"lv":3,"h":"Why clergy marry first; purity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox: if married clergy, you <b>marry before ordination</b></li><li class=\"d1\">unmarried man looking to marry → younger women <b>aren't his sisters</b> → \"all kinds of problems\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Men don't look at <b>sisters</b> the way they look at women who aren't relatives</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Purity</b> in an ancient traditional culture = more than no <b>impure thoughts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>protective</b> of them from men who would</li><li class=\"d1\">protect their <b>honor, dignity, modesty</b></li></ul>"},{"t":593.32,"lv":3,"h":"Family = honor and shame","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Brethren language</b> in Paul and Acts - the family language for the community</li><li class=\"d0\">Not just \"we all love each other\"</li><li class=\"d1\">family in the ancient world = <b>responsibilities</b>: <b>honor and shame</b>, dignity, protecting, caring</li><li class=\"d1\">brother <b>poor and destitute on the street</b> while you live well → <b>humiliation</b>; same for your parents</li><li class=\"d0\">Why Christian communities were <b>amazing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">treated <b>unrelated</b> people as their own family</li><li class=\"d1\">everyone had <b>food, a place to live, clothes</b>; defended, protected their <b>honor</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We've <b>lost</b> this - families <b>atomized</b></li><li class=\"d1\">US: <b>nuclear family</b> = mom, dad, kids; \"sacrosanct\"</li><li class=\"d1\">even at church: <b>\"me and my family\"</b>, my business vs. theirs</li><li class=\"d1\">Romans: <i>pater familias</i>, uncles, aunts, cousins - <b>one big unit</b>, easier to carry into church</li><li class=\"d1\">other guy my <b>brother in Christ</b> → his kids my <b>nieces and nephews</b>, his parents my aunts and uncles; kids <b>\"cousins\"</b></li></ul>"},{"t":841.32,"lv":2,"h":"5:3-4 - Widows who are really widows","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Honor widows who are really widows\"</b>; widow with children or grandchildren → let them <b>first learn piety at home</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Really a widow</b> = has <b>no one</b> to take care of her</li><li class=\"d0\">Children/grandchildren not caring = <b>public disgrace</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't let someone say <b>\"the church will take care of Ma\"</b> and go do his own thing - <b>\"completely immoral\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">violating the <b>commandment</b> by not caring for parents and grandparents</li><li class=\"d0\">Why <b>\"first\"</b>: maybe trying but can't - <b>poverty</b></li><li class=\"d1\">let them do that first → church community <b>makes up the difference</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>not dumb</b> - some will <b>take advantage</b> of the church's love</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you don't have to be dumb\" - not good <b>for them</b>; goal is to get <b>them</b> saved too</li><li class=\"d1\">not caring for mother/grandmother = <b>sin</b> → uncorrected leads to <b>destruction</b>; \"not loving to not correct\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Make sure <b>ma and grandma</b> are cared for - but also save the <b>recalcitrant grandchild</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1023.0,"lv":2,"h":"5:5-7 - The widow devoted to prayer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Now she who is really a widow\"</b> and alone <b>trusts in God</b>, continues in <b>supplications and prayers night and day</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"She who lives in pleasure is dead while she lives\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Widow with no one → <b>dedicates herself to the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>repurposing</b> dedicated virgins at the temple</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Anna</b> at the Feast of the <b>Presentation</b> - widow, lived at the temple, life of <b>prayer</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>female monasticism</b> develops from the OT - one step along the way</li><li class=\"d0\">How a widow <b>supported by the church</b> ought to behave</li><li class=\"d1\">not spending church money on <b>liquor</b>; not <b>\"phandering\"</b> around town</li><li class=\"d0\">Now not the physical temple - within the <b>church community</b> wherever it is</li></ul>"},{"t":1129.12,"lv":3,"h":"The office of widow","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Early church: mention in the <b>canons</b> - an actual <b>office of widow</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes lived <b>at or near the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">did what women at the <b>gates of the tabernacle/temple</b> did: <b>sewing, cleaning</b>, helping</li><li class=\"d1\">church took care of them, they took care of the church - <b>positive relationship</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hard for the widowed (especially <b>widowed young</b>): <b>sense of purpose and place</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul and the church have <b>a place</b> to fit in, contribute, <b>bless others and be blessed</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1207.08,"lv":3,"h":"5:8 - Worse than an unbeliever","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"If anyone does not provide for his own... he has denied the faith and is worse than an unbeliever\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">now talking to the <b>son or grandson</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Good works are required for salvation. I know, scary, huh?\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't care for your family → <b>denied the faith</b> - no matter what you say you believe</li><li class=\"d1\">faithfulness involves the <b>work</b> of caring for those you're responsible for</li><li class=\"d0\">Why <b>worse</b>? Romans 1-2: <b>Judeans</b> worse than Gentiles - they had the <b>Torah</b>, revelation</li><li class=\"d1\">Gentiles obviously sinning but <b>ignorance as excuse</b>; the others don't have one</li><li class=\"d1\">you have the <b>gospel</b> and let mother/grandmother <b>starve</b> → worse than a non-Christian; <b>more given, stronger judgment</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1335.76,"lv":3,"h":"Order of responsibility","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Clear: regardless of what you believe in your heart, <b>not saved</b> if you don't care for those you're responsible for</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>First responsibility</b>: provide for <b>yourself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">said earlier: <b>work with their hands</b>, provide for family, those closest</li><li class=\"d0\">Beyond that: contribute to the <b>extended family</b> of the church</li><li class=\"d0\">In all three the goal is to <b>positively contribute</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"Ask not what the church can do for you, ask what you can do for the church\"</b> - Paul in 1 Timothy</li></ul>"},{"t":1415.8,"lv":2,"h":"5:9-10 - Requirements for the widows' list","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"A widow under 60... taken into the number\"</b>: wife of <b>one man</b>, <b>well reported for good works</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Brought up children; <b>lodged strangers</b>; <b>washed the saints' feet</b>; <b>relieved the afflicted</b>; diligently followed every good work</li><li class=\"d0\">Another list of requirements; one is <b>age: 60</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>canon</b> (an ecumenical council - \"second, I think\") <b>lowered it to 40</b></li><li class=\"d1\">past <b>childbearing years</b> - won't fall in love and <b>run off</b>; commits the rest of her life</li><li class=\"d0\">Like the lists for <b>bishops, presbyters, deacons</b> - here: <b>model Christian woman</b></li><li class=\"d1\">raising children, good reputation, <b>hospitality to travelers</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>washing feet</b> = <b>humble service</b>, lowest job - vs. service that draws attention and gets <b>thanks and praise</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"doing the jobs nobody else wants to do\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1599.48,"lv":2,"h":"5:11-13 - Refuse younger widows","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Refuse the younger widows\"</b> - grow wanton against Christ, desire to marry, <b>cast off their first faith</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Young woman may say \"I'm only <b>28</b>, I'll dedicate myself\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>mortality rate</b> - often widowed very young</li><li class=\"d1\">\"next maybe <b>50 years</b>\" at church - then sees a young guy, <b>leaves</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: don't put them where they'd <b>break their commitment to God and the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not ready until <b>60</b>; don't put a <b>stumbling block</b> in their path</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:13</b>: <b>idle, wandering house to house, gossips and busybodies</b></li><li class=\"d1\">young, <b>lots of energy</b> - not enough church work to keep them occupied</li><li class=\"d1\">woman in her <b>60s-70s</b> with a place, food, things to do = fine; <b>20s</b> = not enough to do</li><li class=\"d1\">better they be <b>out in the world</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1746.64,"lv":2,"h":"5:14 - Let them remarry","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"I desire that younger widows marry, bear children, manage the house\"</b> - give no opportunity to the <b>adversary</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Remarry → <b>busy and occupied</b>, not sitting in <b>idleness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">otherwise <b>Satan tempts</b>: after a <b>young man</b>, leave the church, <b>gossip</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Be <b>fully involved</b> in life</li><li class=\"d0\">Again aiming at her <b>salvation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">commitment then <b>breaking it</b> = bad for her salvation</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>idleness</b> and temptation to gossip = bad for her</li><li class=\"d1\">this is what's good for her salvation → how <b>Timothy</b> should handle it</li></ul>"},{"t":1791.04,"lv":3,"h":"Q: Was remarriage counter-cultural?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: some cultures (<b>India</b>) - widowed, you <b>don't remarry</b>; was it so here?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not really</b> - especially <b>Jewish</b> culture, <b>levirate marriage</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>women remarrying</b> very common</li><li class=\"d0\">Different for men and women</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>widowers</b> remarrying fairly common, esp. with children</li><li class=\"d1\">men + <b>divorce</b> = \"a whole other kettle of fish\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Divorced women</b>: not by their own choice <b>99.9%</b> - they were <b>cut off</b></li><li class=\"d1\">vs. divorced man who <b>put off his wife to marry someone else</b> → treated differently in the Bible</li><li class=\"d0\">Man with <b>young children</b> whose wife died: <b>expected to remarry</b> - \"weird for him not to\", to help raise children</li></ul>"},{"t":1903.28,"lv":3,"h":"New context for the widow's role","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>delineating</b> the role of widows and <b>dedicated virgins at the temple</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like the other roles - <b>preexisted in the Old Testament</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Transition</b>: now churches and communities <b>all over the place</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"how does that work <b>now</b>, in this context?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">laying out how the <b>widow role</b> works in the <b>new context</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:15</b>: <b>\"some have already turned aside after Satan\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not hypothetical - <b>already had problems</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Widows in your family → <b>you relieve them</b>, don't expect the church to</li><li class=\"d1\">church cares for <b>those who really have no one</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2031.08,"lv":2,"h":"5:17 - Double honor for elders","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Elders who rule well... worthy of double honor\"</b>, especially those who <b>labor in word and doctrine</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Presbyters</b> of the new covenant community, like the <b>elders of the people</b> in the OT</li><li class=\"d0\">Especially honored by <b>Timothy and the community</b> - treated with <b>respect</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Especially those directly in <b>preaching and teaching</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2080.52,"lv":3,"h":"5:18 - Do not muzzle the ox","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"You shall not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain\"</b>; <b>\"the laborer is worthy of his wages\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Second time Paul quotes it - earlier to the <b>Corinthians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">For those who divide OT law into <b>ceremonial, moral, civil</b> and say parts don't apply</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul quotes an <b>obscure agricultural law</b> about an ox at the plow → applies it to the church <b>as still binding</b>: <b>\"as it is written\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no weird allegory</b></li><li class=\"d0\">His point: animal serves you → God commands <b>dignity, respect, kindness</b>, feed and care</li><li class=\"d1\">how much more a <b>human</b> serving you and helping you work out your <b>salvation</b> - \"infinitely more\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not mistreat a farm animal → <b>definitely not a human in authority</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Two pieces</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2171.84,"lv":3,"h":"Two pieces: money and honor","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Practical</b>: he's talking about <b>money</b> - take care of their <b>needs</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm a presbyter. I <b>don't have a job</b>\" - <b>not a job</b></li><li class=\"d1\">church meets my needs: <b>rent, food, clothes</b>, place to live → so I can <b>serve this community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">always so in the <b>Old Testament</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Honor and respect</b> - as in the first verse</li><li class=\"d1\">church leaders are <b>servants</b> and use the title</li><li class=\"d1\">some decide that makes them <b>household servants</b>, <b>the maid</b>, <b>employees</b> - \"you work for me\" → treat with the <b>opposite of respect</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul corrects <b>both</b> at once</li></ul>"},{"t":2273.08,"lv":2,"h":"5:19-20 - Accusations against elders","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Do not receive an accusation against an elder except from two or three witnesses\"</b>; those sinning <b>rebuke in the presence of all</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Shift</b>: earlier - older man, <b>don't rebuke publicly</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But a <b>leader/teacher</b> sinning, everyone looking to him as example</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>two or three witnesses</b> → he did or said it <b>publicly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">private sin or mere <b>suspicion</b> - won't have witnesses</li><li class=\"d0\">Then <b>rebuked publicly</b>, since everyone looks to him</li><li class=\"d1\">part of the role: whatever you do <b>publicly</b> is subject to <b>public correction</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if <b>bad enough and unrepentant</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2368.24,"lv":2,"h":"5:21 - Charge before God and the elect angels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"I charge you before God and the Lord Jesus Christ and the elect angels\"</b> - observe without <b>prejudice</b>, nothing with <b>partiality</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Calls <b>witnesses</b> (just said two or three): <b>Father, Christ, elect angels</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Q: <b>elect</b> angels - \"sorry, Calvinists\" - chosen in Christ before foundation of world?</li><li class=\"d1\">angels who <b>stayed faithful</b> - that's what makes them elect</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>God and his whole council</b> - like the <b>last judgment</b> scene with Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Timothy must <b>judge the way God judges</b>: no partiality</li><li class=\"d1\">don't let <b>friends off easy</b>; don't go hard on people you don't like</li><li class=\"d1\">don't let the <b>wealthy</b> off, go harder on those who <b>can't do anything for you</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2493.72,"lv":3,"h":"Laying on hands; sharing sins","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Do not lay hands on anyone hastily\"</b> - not attacking; <b>ordaining</b> into an office</li><li class=\"d1\">he gave the <b>criteria</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wrong person → not good for the <b>church</b>, not good for <b>that person</b></li><li class=\"d1\">churches have felt pain from people who <b>shouldn't have been ordained</b></li><li class=\"d1\">those people in pain too - often <b>whole faith shipwrecked</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul always looking at <b>both</b> - <b>salvation of everybody</b>; no sides</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"God wills that all men be saved\"</b> (back at the beginning)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Don't share in other people's sins\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just don't do - don't give <b>approval</b>, <b>don't let things slide</b></li><li class=\"d1\">leader <b>participates by allowing it</b>; <b>Corinth</b> incest - \"you've got to say something\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2613.76,"lv":2,"h":"5:23 - A little wine","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"No longer drink only water, but use a little wine for your stomach's sake and your frequent infirmities\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Very personal</b> - Timothy's <b>gastrointestinal issues</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not a proof text</b> against people who don't drink</li><li class=\"d1\">not an <b>article of faith</b> that we don't drink - never was</li><li class=\"d1\">for some <b>Protestants</b> today; can't get it from OT or NT</li><li class=\"d1\">one more piece of evidence</li></ul>"},{"t":2661.08,"lv":2,"h":"5:24-25 - Evident and hidden sins","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Some men's sins are clearly evident, preceding them to judgment\"</b>, others <b>follow later</b>; good works likewise evident, others <b>cannot be hidden</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Timothy <b>judging and administering</b>, dealing with sin in the community</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Obvious sins</b>: infamous, everybody knows</li><li class=\"d1\">easy to <b>\"lower the boom\"</b> - community probably already <b>ostracized</b> them</li><li class=\"d0\">Others: <b>just as many, just as serious</b> sins - nobody knows</li><li class=\"d1\">not public ≠ less serious, less a <b>threat to salvation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So Timothy must use <b>caution</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2739.08,"lv":3,"h":"The church's imbalance, and the leader's task","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Verse \"I wish more people responsible for <b>preaching and teaching</b> would <b>meditate on</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Last <b>half century</b>: church good at <b>publicly condemning</b> public sins of some groups</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not incorrectly</b> - what they call sin <b>is</b> sin</li><li class=\"d0\">Bad at <b>rooting out</b> less public sins</li><li class=\"d1\">until later made public → <b>big public scandal</b>; never addressed before it</li><li class=\"d1\">other sins and other people <b>constantly, loudly condemned</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul counsels <b>against precisely that</b> - neither helps the sinner find <b>salvation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hidden sin not addressed: no closer to Christ because <b>hasn't been found out</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Leader: address the <b>flagrant</b> in a way that draws to <b>repentance</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Get to know people, spend the time</b>, find who has non-public sins</li><li class=\"d1\">deal with them <b>before</b> they become a massive public scandal, for all involved</li></ul>"}]});
